<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fuk'anggan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1753–<a href="/years/1796">1796</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fuk'anggan (1753–1796) was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> general and official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a>. He suppressed the Lin Shuangwen rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1787–1788 and in 1792 led a Qing army across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-nepalese-war">Sino-Nepalese War</a>, advancing close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> and forcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> to accept peace and send regular tribute missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. He died campaigning against the Miao rebellion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fukanggan.jpg" alt="Fuk'anggan" width="320" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qing dynasty painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fuk%27anggan_(%E7%A6%8F%E5%BA%B7%E5%AE%89).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
